{ The Foundation of Chess Improvement: Move Safety

If you strip chess down to its core, one principle stands above all: move safety. No amount of strategic understanding, endgame technique, or deep opening knowledge will matter if you consistently make unsafe moves. While it’s tempting to focus on executing brilliant attacks or maneuvering pieces with finesse, the reality is that most games—especially at the amateur level—are decided by tactical blunders.

Good players are not necessarily those who find brilliant moves, but those who make fewer mistakes. This course is built on that truth. If you can raise your floor—eliminating reckless blunders, ensuring move safety, and maintaining a solid baseline of play—you will immediately see results, even without increasing your chess knowledge.

Many players focus too much on improving their best moves rather than removing their worst ones. However, chess is not about finding genius moves every turn—it’s about not losing the game before your skills can shine. Imagine a player who can calculate five moves ahead but consistently blunders a piece every few games. Their calculation skills become meaningless if they never get the chance to use them.

The Goal of This Course

This course is designed to help you stop giving away games due to crude tactical blunders by teaching a structured approach to blunder-checking and move safety. You’ll learn:

  *  How to spot and eliminate unsafe moves before playing them.

  *  A simple, actionable blunder-checking process to ensure your moves are safe.

  *  How to apply checklists and practical techniques to improve your decision-making.

  *  How to counteract cognitive biases that lead to impulsive mistakes.

    By the end of this course, you won’t just be avoiding blunders—you’ll also develop the tactical awareness to recognize and punish your opponent’s mistakes more effectively. Instead of playing hope chess—making moves and crossing your fingers that your opponent won’t notice a mistake—you’ll cultivate the habit of verifying your moves before committing to them. A safer player is a stronger player. And by mastering move safety, you’ll build a solid foundation for every other aspect of your chess improvement journey. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

