{ In this course, a blunder is defined as a move that changes the position in a way that creates a winning tactical opportunity for your opponent. This is different from simply failing to spot the opponent’s existing tactical threat. Instead, it’s about your own move actively making the position worse. This definition aligns with NM Dan Heisman’s 'Is Your Move Safe?' concept—a systematic approach to evaluating the safety of each move before playing it.

We thus need to make a key distinction between two processes:

  *  Missing a threat – Failing to see an opponent’s move that creates a tactical problem for you.

  *  Committing a blunder – Your own move alters the position in a way that allows your opponent a decisive tactical shot.

    This course focuses on the second step—ensuring that your own moves do not blunder the game away. If you’re looking to improve at spotting your opponent’s threats, I’ve already created a dedicated course for that: 'My Opponent’s Last Move: Identifying Threats, Mistakes, and Misconceptions': [https://www.chessable.com/my-opponents-move-identifying-threats-mistakes-and-misconceptions/course/163950/]

    Blunder-checking is a deliberate process to ensure that your moves do not allow winning tactics for your opponent. Unlike quick exercises such as Puzzle Rush, which train you to recognize tactics instantly, blunder-checking requires a mental shift: Instead of searching for the best move for yourself, you focus on the strongest reply your opponent can make after your intended move.

    This change in perspective cultivates a scientific mindset and counters wishful thinking, which is a major source of blunders. We will talk about the cognitive biases and how they cause blunders in the later sections. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

