{ Humans rely on cognitive shortcuts to process information efficiently. But in chess, these shortcuts can lead to devastating blunders. Recognizing and counteracting these biases is essential for improving your blunder-checking process. In this section, we’ll focus on confirmation bias—one of the most common culprits behind chess mistakes.

Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek information that supports our beliefs while disregarding evidence that contradicts them. In chess, this manifests when a player locks onto a move and looks for reasons it works, rather than testing for flaws.

Wishful thinking is a form of confirmation bias. NM Dan Heisman coined the term 'Hope Chess' to describe this phenomenon—where a player moves without verifying whether their opponent has a strong response. When the opponent doesn’t cooperate with our hopes and instead punishes the move, our position can collapse instantly.

Overreliance on tactical puzzles can sometimes reinforce a confirmation bias mentality. In most puzzle-based training, your good-looking moves almost always work—you’re rewarded for finding the intuitive solution rather than being challenged to question your first assumptions. This might explain why many players boast extraordinarily high puzzle ratings but struggle with much lower ELO ratings in actual games.

Chess is one of the most unforgiving sports. A single blunder can erase all of your previously built advantages, instantly turning a winning position into a loss. Unlike most other sports, where a dominant lead provides a cushion—allowing top players to rest on the bench, as in basketball—chess offers no such safety net. No matter how well you've played up to that point, one careless move can end the game on the spot.

As NM Dan Heisman puts it: If you miss a winning tactic for yourself, the game continues and you may get another chance. But if you blunder, the game might end immediately. This harsh reality indicates why raising your floor is just as important as improving your ceiling.

To guard against blunders, you must adopt a scientific approach to evaluating your moves—one that prioritizes refutation over validation. Scientists don’t try to prove their hypotheses correct; they actively seek out ways to disprove them, testing for weaknesses to ensure their conclusions hold up under scrutiny. In chess, you must do the same: instead of looking for reasons why your move works, search for reasons why it might fail.

This mindset shift—from self-validation to self-skepticism—transforms your decision-making. When you assume your opponent will always find the best response, you force yourself to think critically, checking for tactical refutations and hidden threats before committing to a move. This process creates an internal blunder-checking system that keeps your moves grounded in reality.

Much like a well-tested scientific theory, a move that survives rigorous scrutiny is far more reliable than one accepted at face value. The more you challenge your own ideas, the fewer blunders you’ll make—and the stronger, more resilient your chess will become. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

