{ A key feature of this course is the carefully chosen positions you’ll encounter. These aren’t random examples — each position has been thoughtfully designed to maximize your learning.

I’ve drawn inspiration from real blunders made by players at all levels, grounding the material in real-world scenarios. However, to make these positions as educational as possible, I’ve modified most positions by removing unnecessary details and focusing on the most important elements. This follows the principles of cognitive load theory, which emphasizes reducing distractions so your brain can focus on the core idea without being overwhelmed.

The process is similar to Plato’s concept of an ideal world, where the essence of an idea is distilled into its purest form. Here, the positions are simplified to highlight the key concepts clearly and effectively. This approach ensures you can concentrate on what really matters, making your learning experience focused and impactful.

The positions you’ll encounter in this course are the result of continuous refinement and real-world testing with my students. Each position has been meticulously crafted and then rigorously tested in live chess lessons. The feedback from these sessions has been invaluable.

As my students worked through these positions, their reactions and the mistakes they made provided crucial insights. I paid close attention to the types of errors that recurred and the aspects of the positions that caused the most difficulty. This iterative process allowed me to fine-tune the positions, ensuring they target the most important elements and learning points.

Incorporating student feedback not only improved the clarity and focus of the positions, but also highlighted common mistakes. These mistakes have been annotated in the course, providing a comprehensive guide to avoid them. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

