{ In this course, a loose piece is defined as a piece with an equal number of attackers and defenders. For example:

  *  A piece with no attackers and no defenders is loose.

  *  A piece with one attacker and one defender is still loose.

    A hanging piece, on the other hand, has more attackers than defenders. You can create a hanging piece in several ways:

  *  Removing the defender of a loose piece, leaving it unprotected. These are covered in the chapter 'Loose Pieces and Loose Squares' [/course/258968/9].

  *  Moving a piece to a square where it can be immediately captured. These are covered in the chapter 'Not Hanging Material' [/course/258968/6].

  *  Uncovering an enemy attack by moving a piece that was shielding against it. These are covered in the chapter 'Seeing the Snipers.' [/course/258968/7].

    For example, if you move the defender of a loose piece, you instantly turn it into a hanging piece.

    Importantly, squares can also be “loose” or “hanging” when they become vulnerable to tactical threats. For instance, an unprotected square might allow a back-rank mate. This course will guide you in identifying these subtleties and integrating them into your decision-making. Concrete examples will be explored in depth in relevant chapters. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

