{ One of the most fundamental milestones in improvement is developing the habit of never leaving your pieces en prise—placing them on squares where they can be directly captured. This chapter will train you to eliminate these blunders and develop the habit of moving with care.

A hanging piece can arise in three ways:

  *  Moving a piece to a square where it can be directly captured.

  *  Uncovering an enemy attack by moving a piece that was shielding against it.

  *  Removing the defender of a loose piece, turning it into a hanging piece.

    This chapter focuses on the first way—directly placing a piece on a vulnerable square. You will be given puzzles where your task is to deliver a check within a set number of moves—ranging from two or six—without allowing your piece to be captured along the way. Unlike other chapters, you will be allowed to move the same piece multiple times in a row, but with a crucial condition: you must ensure that your piece cannot be captured along the way by the enemy pieces.

    These exercises will sharpen your board vision, forcing you to track enemy threats and piece placements constantly. To succeed, you must consciously scan the entire board, breaking tunnel vision and ensuring that every move you make is safe.

    A key technique in this chapter is reverse engineering. Instead of moving forward blindly, start by identifying the final square where you can safely deliver a check. Then, work backward to find the only sequence of safe moves that leads you there. This approach will not only help you navigate the board safely, but also develop in you a crucial skill for maneuvering pieces effectively in your future games.

    In all exercises in this chapter, you will be playing with the white pieces, but the white king will be missing from the board. You will only have one piece to move in each position.

    This setup makes it easier to recognize these exercises when reviewing your moves later. You'll quickly associate these positions with the 'Not Hanging Material' chapter, where you're expected to make multiple moves in a row.

    To guide you through each exercise, I have included prompts indicating how many consecutive moves you should make. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

