1... Bxh5 {[%mdl 32768]} 
{ How should White recapture the bishop on h5? } 2. Qxh5! {[%cal Rc4h4,Rg4h5][%csl Rh4]} { The only correct recapture, as White is mindful of the alignment on the 4th rank. } ( 2.gxh5 $4 { This blunder is highly instructive as it shows the danger of focusing too narrowly on one part of the board. White fixates on the alignment along the g-file and pins the black queen, confident of victory. But just when you think the game is won, disaster strikes—highlighting the importance of scanning the whole board to catch hidden threats that can turn the tables in an instant. } 2...Rxh4 { Ouch! This is a painful oversight.

Just when White believed Black was on the verge of resigning due to the pinned queen, Black delivers a surprise checkmate! This example highlights the dramatic impact of pawn recaptures, which can open up new lines of attack on files, ranks, and diagonals. White needed to view the entire board and recognize the horizontal alignment along the 4th rank—a critical detail that was missed in the excitement of the moment. } ) *

