[SourceTitle "https://www.chessable.com/variation/48786882"]

1. -- { The Blunder Check framework follows a logical hierarchy that mirrors the natural progression of tactical priorities in a chess game. Checks (C) come first because overlooking a checkmate threat can result in an immediate loss—a blunder of the highest order. While I generally advise against mindlessly analyzing every check, capture, and threat, I do recommend deliberately scanning for checks, as checkmate ends the game and is much more concrete than evaluating vague 'threats.'

Recognizing and understanding essential checkmate patterns is a key skill in preventing blunders, and this chapter will introduce a few fundamental patterns that frequently decide games. A crucial auxiliary question in this chapter is: 'Can they attack my newly moved piece?' This question is particularly relevant when moving your king—before committing to a king move, you must ensure that it won’t land in an immediate tactical disaster.

The question 'Can they give a devastating check?' becomes even more critical when your king is exposed (lacking pawn cover) or restricted in mobility (having no safe escape squares). This chapter explores positions where these factors play a decisive role.

Additionally, we will connect these ideas to the 'Seeing the Snipers' chapter, as long-range pieces like bishops and queens can strike from across the board, delivering unexpected and devastating checks that often go unnoticed.

As always, I also included positions where you must engage in short but precise calculations to justify a move that initially appears to be a blunder. This approach trains you to avoid lazy, automatic rejections of moves that seem unsafe at first glance, helping you develop a more disciplined decision-making process while also simulating a real game environment. } {[%mdl 32768]} *

[Event "Preventing Blunders in Chess"]
