[FEN "3rkr2/6pp/p1pQ1n2/1p2pP1q/4P3/7P/PPP3P1/R4RK1 w - - 1 25"]
[SourceTitle "https://www.chessable.com/variation/42576205"]

25. Qxe5+ {[%mdl 32768]} { Black has an extra piece here, but the white queen just gave a check. How should Black respond to this check? } Kd7! {[%cal Re8f7,Re5e6][%csl Re6,Rf7]} { The only move that avoids checkmate! Black uses the method of elimination here. Black had only two choices with the king, and the alternative was allowing a checkmate on the next turn. } ( 25...Kf7 $4 { This would be a blunder as it allows a checkmate: } 26.Qe6 { Oops! White had two different checks against 25...Kf7⁇, but this is clearly the deadlier one! Perhaps Black had only focused on } ( 26.Qc7+? { which would allow the black king to escape into safety after } 26...Kg8 $17 { We cannot expect our opponents to cooperate with us! } ) ) 
26. Rad1+ { This check looks devastating at first sight, but the king will survive: } Kc8 {[%cal Gc8c7,Gc8b7,Gc8b8][%csl Gc8]} { There is no checkmate in sight! The black king has several escape squares from here, such as c7, b7 and b8. } 
27. Qe6+ Kc7 { This is the most ambitious choice. The other two alternatives gave White a perpetual check: } ( 27...Kb7 28.Qe7+ Kc8 29.Qe6+ Kb7 30.Qe7+ $10 ) ( 27...Kb8 28.Rxd8+ Rxd8 29.Qxc6 $10 { White has the perpetual check with Qb6+ & Qc6+, etc. } ) 
28. Qe7+ Rd7! {[%cal Gd7d1,Gh5d1,Re7f8]} 
29. Rxd7+ ( 29.Qxf8? { Grabbing the rook on f8 would drop the rook on d1: } 29...Rxd1 30.Qxg7+ Nd7 { Black will ask some questions in this position. } ) Nxd7 {[%cal Gd7f8][%csl Gd7,Rf8]} 
30. Qxg7 Qe2 {[%csl Rc2,Re4,Gc7,Ge2]} { Black stands a bit better here despite White having 3 pawns for the knight. Black's king is very secure and the black queen is putting pressure on the weak pawns.

What did we learn from this example?

We once again saw the critical importance of the checklist question: 'Am I allowing a check as a result of my next move?' In this case, the king on f7 was vulnerable to two different checks, one of which was checkmate! Focusing solely on the less dangerous check for the opponent would result in a blunder. Therefore, it's essential to consider all possible choices for the opponent and evaluate every potential check against our move.

Another interesting aspect of this position was that the correct move, 25...Kd7!, initially appeared more risky because it placed the king on an open file, allowing a potential rook check with 26. Rd1. A common mistake would be to dismiss this move outright based on general principles (Dan Heisman refers to this error as 'handwaving').

Conversely, the blunder 25...Kf7⁇ seemed safer at first glance, as it moved the king toward the perceived safety of the kingside. This example shows the importance of concrete calculation and analysis rather than over-relying on general principles and superficial features in chess. The checklist questions can aid in this concrete analysis, encouraging you to look beyond general assumptions.

The importance of asking 'Am I allowing a check?' becomes even more crucial when our king is exposed. In such situations, it's vital to slow down and carefully evaluate all possible checks that the opponent might exploit. Moreover, the broader question, 'Can they attack my newly-moved piece?' was also valuable in this exercise. This question helps to ensure that any potential threats against the piece you've just moved, including your king, are carefully considered, reducing the risk of overlooking an attack from your opponent.

Finally, this position also demonstrated the value of using the method of elimination. Once you correctly identified that 25. Kf7⁇ was a blunder, you should quickly play the only viable alternative, 25...Kd7, thereby conserving precious time on the clock. When you realize there's only one alternative that doesn't allow mate on the next turn, you can play it rapidly without needing extensive calculation.

} 
*

