34... h3+ 
{ White has an extra pawn in this endgame. How should the white king respond to this check? } 35. Kf1! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Ra8a1,Gf1e2][%csl Ra1]} { The only move that avoids a checkmate! White anticipates ...Ra1+ and creates an escape square for the king on e2. } ( 35.Kg1 $4 { All other options would lead to checkmate: } 35...Ra1+! { White had to anticipate this check before choosing the location for their king. } 36.Rc1 Rxc1 ) ( 35.Kh1 $4 Ra1+ 36.Rc1 Rxc1 ) Ra1+ { Now this check is no longer dangerous: } 
36. Ke2 { The white king is very safe. } Rh1 { This move looks dangerous at first sight, as Black will soon create a passed pawn on h3. However, everything is under control, which is also due to the buried knight on g6. } 
37. Nh6 {[%cal Gh6g4]} Rxh2 
38. Rc1 {[%cal Rh2h1]} { Cutting off the enemy rook! } Rg2 
39. Nxg4 h2 
40. Rh1 $18  {[%csl Rh2,Gh1]} { White will win the h2-pawn and obtain a winning edge.

This example highlights the crucial role of mastering essential checkmate patterns. The back-rank mate is one of the most common and recurring checkmate motifs. Recognizing it instantly would have helped White avoid the blunder with ease. } *

