[SourceTitle "https://www.chessable.com/variation/42748544"]

38... Rg5+ 
{ White currently has an extra rook and knight, but the white king has to react to this check now. What should White play? } 39. Kh2! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Gg1h3,Rf6h6][%csl Gg1,Gh3]} { The only move that wins, and the other two alternatives were allowing a mate in 1! The knight on g1 will cover the white king now. } ( 39.Kh3 $4 { Both alternatives were allowing checkmate to the same move: } 39...Qh6 { Lawnmower Mate - also known as the Corridor Mate. This classic checkmate pattern involves two major pieces — typically two rooks or a rook and a queen — that push the enemy king toward the edge of the board and checkmate it. } ) ( 39.Kh4 $4 Qh6 { Lawnmower Mate again! } ) Qh6+ ( 39...Rh5+ { This check will be answered in the same way: } 40.Nh3! { In the absence of pawn cover, the knight nicely defends the white king. } 40...Rxh3+ { This is the only check that has to be calculated: } 41.Kxh3 Qh6+ { A single queen won't be enough to save the perpetual check: } 42.Kg2 Qg5+ 43.Kh1! { Leaving the h2-square for the white rook, anticipating the check on the h-file: } 43...Qh4+ 44.Rh2 $18 { White pins and wins. } ) 
40. Nh3 {[%csl Gh3,Gh2]} { Knights can be great defenders of their king. Now the only meaningful checks left to Black are rook and queen sacrifices, but they won't work. White is winning with their huge material advantage.

Given that the white king was exposed and lacked pawn cover in the initial position, the 'C' in our checklist (Am I allowing a devastating check as a result of my next move?) became crucial. White needed to carefully consider all potential checks along the h-file, as the king had to remain on that file.

Just like the previous example with the back-rank mate, this position shows the importance of mastering essential checkmate patterns to avoid blunders. With the white king exposed in the corner, White needed to anticipate that Black was on the verge of delivering a Lawnmower Mate with their heavy pieces. Recognizing this pattern would have made it easier for White to spot the only defensive resource to prevent checkmate. } *

