44... Qh3+ 
{ White has two ways to respond against this check. Which is the correct one? } 45. Qh2! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rh1g1,Rc7b6][%csl Rh1,Rb6,Rg1]} { The only move! White avoids the blunder by not placing their king on a square that would allow a devastating check. } ( 45.Kg1 $4 { This would be a blunder as it allows a deadly check: } 45...Bb6+! { Oops! The sniper bishop brings devastation with this check. White has no good ways to block this check, and the king has no escape squares. } 46.c5 { This does not change anything. } 46...Bxc5+ { White must give up the queen, and Black will checkmate in 7 moves from here. } ) Qxh2+ ( 45...Qd7 { This is a better move for Black, however White keeps the advantage due to the extra pawn. } ) 
46. Kxh2 Bxf4+ 
47. Kh3 {[%cal Ge1e8]} { And given the Re8# threat, Black cannot capture the g5-pawn. } Kf8 ( 47...Bxg5 $4 { would also allow a devastating check: } 48.Re8 ) 
48. Rf1! {[%cal Gf1f7][%csl Rf7,Rf4]} Bxg5 
49. Rxf7+ Ke8 
50. Rxh7 $18  {[%cal Gh6h7][%csl Gh6]} { The passed pawn on h6 gives White a winning edge in this endgame.

What did we learn from this example?

Checks are the most forcing moves in chess, and checkmate ends the game. Therefore, when performing a blunder-check, the top priority should be to ask: 'Am I allowing a check as a result of my next move?'. Had White considered this question, they would have avoided the blunder. By playing 45. Kg1⁇, they moved their king to a square that the enemy bishop could attack.

As we studied in the chapter 'Seeing the Snipers', sniper bishops from the other side of the board can easily be overlooked, especially by beginners. This example also serves as a valuable test of board vision. By consistently applying this checklist question, we can enhance our board awareness and combat tunnel vision by scanning the entire board for potential checks before making our move.

This puzzle also teaches us the method of elimination: once White recognized that 45. Kg1⁇ was a blunder, they should have used the method of elimination and played 45. Qh2—the only viable alternative—without wasting too much time on the clock.

Finally, the broader checklist question 'Can they attack my newly-moved piece?' is also valuable for this puzzle. The white king, having just moved to g1, was vulnerable to an attack from the sniper bishop. } *

