{ As explained in the Introduction, this course distinguishes between loose and hanging pieces:

A loose piece has an equal number of attackers and defenders.

  *  A piece with no attackers and no defenders is loose.

  *  A piece with one attacker and one defender is still loose.

    A hanging piece has more attackers than defenders, making it immediately vulnerable to capture. A piece can become hanging in three ways:

  *  Moving a piece to a square where it can be directly captured.

  *  Uncovering an enemy attack by moving a piece that was shielding against it.

  *  Removing the defender of a loose piece, turning it into a hanging piece.

    This chapter focuses on the third way—removing the defender of a loose piece or allowing your opponent to do so. You'll also learn how to recognize situations where your opponent can exploit a loose piece with a deadly double attack. These tactical patterns—removing the defender and double attacks—will be the core themes of this chapter.

    One of the key lessons in this chapter is the importance of always being aware of the loose pieces in your camp. Recognizing loose pieces before they become hanging pieces is a crucial habit that will save you from unnecessary blunders and improve your defensive awareness.

    Beyond avoiding blunders, this chapter will challenge you to calculate short variations where seemingly unsafe moves can actually work with precise play. This will simulate real game conditions, helping you develop disciplined calculation skills and preventing the habit of dismissing moves too quickly just because they look risky at first glance.

    Additionally, just like pieces, squares can also be loose or hanging. An unprotected square can become a tactical liability, leading to threats like back-rank mates or forks. In the section on loose squares, you will also reinforce the lessons from the Seeing the Snipers [/course/258968/7] chapter. Previously, you learned how moving a piece can expose an attack on another piece. Here, you'll extend that concept to squares — forming an understanding of how your move can expose a critical square to enemy threats. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} *

