5... dxe4 {[%mdl 32768]} 
{ How should White recapture the pawn on e4? } 6. dxe4! {[%cal Rg2e4,Rc8h3][%csl Rh3]} { The only recapture that is not a blunder! The bishop on g2 was glued defending the knight on h3. } ( 6.Bxe4 $4 { This recapture is a blunder as it unprotects the loose knight on h3 and allows } 6...Bxh3 { Oops! Black grabs the now-hanging knight on h3. Before the bishop on g2 moved, the knight on h3 was a loose piece: it was attacked once (by the bishop on c8) and guarded only once (by the bishop on g2). The moment the g2-bishop captured on e4, the safety of the h3-knight was compromised, leaving it as a hanging piece.

What did we learn from this example?

Loose pieces play a crucial role in tactics, making 'L' (Loose Pieces) an important part of our blunder-check checklist. We should always ask ourselves: 'Am I creating a loose piece as a result of my next move?'. This question ties into the broader inquiry, 'What am I unprotecting as a result of my next move?'. This is a key question for avoiding blunders since every move in chess comes with a potential drawback, and it's vital to consider which pieces and squares we will no longer be protecting after we move.

In this example, it's also important to quickly identify loose pieces (like the knight on h3) and recognize that the bishop on g2 was tied down to guard the knight. This made the bishop overworked, and unable to perform other tasks, such as capturing the pawn on e4. While 6. Bxe4⁇ did not create a loose piece, it turned a loose piece into a hanging piece. This falls under the 'L' category in the checklist, as it directly relates to the concept of loose pieces in chess.

One challenge with this puzzle is the presence of the sniper bishop on c8, positioned across the board. Many players, especially beginners, may overlook such bishops, particularly when there are several pieces nearby that crowd the visual field.

Cognitive scientist and chess player Dr. Benjamin Balas has written insightful blog posts on this topic, discussing the limitations of our visual system and how these limitations can lead to blunders on the chessboard. I highly recommend his work. We also recorded a podcast episode together. By using deliberate checklist questions and regularly asking them during the game, you can help counter these visual limitations and improve your board vision. As you grow stronger, your perceptual system will start to chunk information more effectively, helping you spot those sniper bishops more quickly. } ) *

