{ White is currently up a pawn, but the knight on e5 is attacked by the rook on e8. Should White protect this knight with f2-f4 or Bd4? } 13. f4! {[%cal Rc6c5,Gf4e5][%csl Ge5,Gc5,Rb7,Rc6,Gf4]} { Avoiding the blunder! Pawns are the best defenders in chess because they are the cheapest units to perform that duty. White not only maintains the extra pawn but also keeps the bishop on c5, effectively burying the black bishop on b7 by blocking the c6-pawn. } ( 13.Bd4 $4 { This is an instructive blunder. White is creating a loose piece as a result of this move. Now the knight on e5 became a loose piece, as it is attacked once and defended only once. The problem for White is that Black can remove the defender on the next turn: } 13...c5! { Punishing the blunder! Black simply removes the defender of the loose knight on e5. The white bishop can no longer guard the e5-knight, allowing Black to win material.

This highlights why pawns are often better defenders than pieces in chess: when a piece defends, that piece can be attacked or chased away by other pieces or pawns, as seen in this example. In this particular case, the move ...c6-c5 exemplifies the concept of 'AWL' (Attack With Lower-Valued Piece) using Dan Heisman's terminology. The pawn, being a lower-valued unit than the bishop, forces the bishop to respond to the attack, displacing it and leaving the knight vulnerable.

Let's discuss the checklist questions that could have prevented the blunder in this example. The 'L' part of the checklist is particularly relevant: 'Am I creating a Loose Piece as a result of my next move?'. In fact, 13. Bd4⁇ created two loose pieces—the bishop on d4 and the knight on e5. This move left both pieces vulnerable to attack, allowing Black to win material.

The broader checklist question 'Can they attack my newly moved piece?' is also crucial here. The bishop just moved from c5 to d4, and on the next turn, it would be attacked by the pawn on c5 - which was made possible only because White had moved the bishop! Anticipating such attacks and the drawbacks of our moves is key to avoiding blunders.

Another instructive aspect of this example is the positional strength of the bishop on c5. It was excellently placed, keeping the b7-bishop buried by blocking the c6-pawn. The moment White broke this positional domination by moving the bishop to d4, they were tactically punished. This scenario is a perfect illustration of the saying: 'Tactics flow from good positions in chess.' By maintaining strong positional play, White could have avoided the tactical trap and preserved their advantage. } ) ( 13.Nd3 { This was not a bad move but it allows Black to win the pawn on e4: } 13...Nxe4 14.Rae1 { Black can save the knight on e4 with } 14...Nf6 15.Rxe8+ Nxe8 16.Re1 Bf6 { Here, White stands better due to positional domination: notice how the bishop on c5 is burying the black bishop on b7! } ) *

