{ How should White recapture the pawn on e5? } 17. Bxe5! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Gf3e5][%csl Rg7,Ge5]} { Avoiding the blunder! The white bishop on e5 became a loose piece as a result of this capture, however Black cannot punish it. Please contrast this with the other capture. White challenges the bishop on g7 and thus removes Black's bishop pair. That will give them a slight edge in this endgame. } ( 17.Nxe5 $4 { This would be a blunder, as White is creating a loose piece on e5, which is tying down the bishop on f4 to its defense. And this allows Black to go for a removing the defender tactic: } 17...g5! { Removing the defender of the loose knight on e5! Notice how the bishop on f4 cannot defend the knight on e5 anymore. White is losing a piece, e.g. } 18.Bxg5 Bxe5 $19 { Black wins with an extra bishop.

The instructive aspect of this position lies in the consequences of both 17. Bxe5 and 17. Nxe5, each of which left a loose piece on e5. However, Black had no effective response to 17. Bxe5 because they couldn't dislodge the defensive knight on f3.

On the other hand, the issue with 17. Nxe5 was that the knight, lacking viable targets on that square, became a vulnerable piece due to the bishop on g7. Additionally, the bishop on f4 had no better square to defend the knight, making it susceptible to an 'AWL'—namely, the pawn on g5. } ) *

