{ To err is human. Even super Grandmasters blunder. And sometimes terribly. You would not believe what Aronian played in this position, in an online rapid event in 2021. } 1. Ba3 $4  {[%cal Gd5d2,Gc1a3][%csl Gc1,Rd2]} { Positionally, it looks like a beautiful move. The unopposed bishop finds a lovely diagonal. However, there is a little problem with this move. } Qd2 {[%cal Rc1a3,Gd5d2][%csl Gc1,Ra3,Gd2,Re1]} { Ouch! Would you believe the great Aronian has just blundered a mate in one? You could blame the screens, mouse, and computers, as this was an online game, but there's something more interesting about this blunder that’s worth exploring.

First, notice how Aronian played what seemed to be a beautiful positional move. Strategically, Ba3 was a move begging to be played—it made perfect sense in the context of the position. However, no matter how grand our strategic plans may be, they must always be checked against fundamental blunder-check processes. After all, a simple blunder can ruin even the most well-thought-out strategy, as it did in this case. Ba3⁇ allowed a checkmate on d2, ending the game immediately.

What’s also intriguing about this position is that Black’s target wasn’t a piece, but a specific square: d2. In chess, attacks aren’t always directed at pieces or pawns; sometimes, the target is an empty square. The d2-square was attacked by the black queen and the pawn on c3, while White was defending it with the king and the bishop on c1. When the bishop moved to a3, it left the d2-square unprotected, which then became the decisive mating square.

This shows the importance of the broader checklist question 'What am I unprotecting as a result of my next move?'. Had White considered this, they might have recognized the need to keep the d2-square protected by the bishop.

Additionally, the question 'Am I allowing a Check ('C') as a result of my next move?' would have been crucial here, provided White noticed that the black queen was targeting the d2-square.

By playing Ba3⁇, White allowed a deadly check and converted the 'loose' square on d2 into a 'hanging' square. While it may sound unconventional to talk about loose and hanging squares, it ties nicely into the broader concept of blunder-checks. Before the bishop on c1 moved to a3, the d2-square was loose, being attacked twice and guarded twice. Once the bishop moved, the d2-square became 'hanging,' as the black queen could safely infiltrate and deliver checkmate. } ( 1...Ne3 $4 { Was Aronian only analyzing this reply before he moved the bishop away from c1-h6 diagonal? We will never know. And I don't want to confront him with this question after such a loss... } 2.Qe7 { It is Black's turn to say Ouch! } ) 
