1. -- { This chapter introduces the Alignment (A) concept, a fundamental theme that governs many chess tactics. Alignments occur when pieces are positioned in ways that create tactical opportunities—whether along ranks, files, diagonals, or within a knight’s or a pawn’s range.

This chapter teaches the skill of being aware of alignments before making a move. Before making a move, we must ensure that we are not inadvertently creating an exploitable alignment for our opponent, while also being vigilant about the existing ones on the board. Many blunders arise from overlooking these subtle relationships between pieces.

The key tactical themes associated with alignments are pins, skewers, and discovered attacks. In this course, I will also include knight forks and pawn forks in this alignment category.

Initially, I considered separating knight forks and pawn forks into their own category due to their distinct geometric patterns. However, I ultimately grouped them under Alignments because they exploit specific geometric relationships between pieces. Unlike queen forks, which can target two loose pieces anywhere on the board with varied geometry, knight and pawn forks follow more rigid patterns. A knight fork occurs within its unique movement range, while a pawn fork only arises when two enemy pieces are aligned horizontally with exactly one file separating them. Recognizing these alignment-based vulnerabilities is more instructive than memorizing individual fork patterns in isolation.

In this chapter, we will also explore three important knight geometries that will help you quickly assess fork threats and develop practical rules of thumb to improve your intuition against knights. By understanding these key patterns—Knight Check Shadow, Karpov Distance, and Close Proximity Alignments—you will learn how to position your pieces safely and recognize when a knight is poised for a tactical strike.

This chapter begins by exploring vertical, diagonal, and horizontal alignments before transitioning to knight forks and pawn forks. Each section starts with an instructional example, where you’ll read through the solution and understand the key steps involved. Immediately afterward, you’ll encounter a puzzle on the same theme, allowing you to apply what you just learned to a new position that tests the same underlying skill. } {[%mdl 32768]} *

