Two things went wrong for White when they fell for this blunder: 1) They did not expect the best move from the opponent and casually removed the defender of the bishop on g4, expecting to win that piece on the next turn. 2) They did not pay attention to the alignment on the e-file. The white king and the black rook on e8 were aligned on that file, and White's blunder made this very relevant, as it allowed the e-file to be opened. } ( 12...Qxf6? { All other recaptures would lose the bishop for Black: } 13.Nxg4 $18 ) ( 12...Bxf6? 13.Nxg4 $18 ) ( 12...Rxf6? 13.Nxg4 $18 { White was wishing for these recaptures before capturing the knight on f6! But our opponents don't always grant our wishes in chess!

What did we learn from this example?

This is an excellent example to highlight the 'Alignment' ('A') part of our checklist. In the initial position, the rook on e8 and the white king on e1 were aligned on the e-file, although the pawn on e7 and the knight on e5 were in the way of those two pieces. This alignment played a crucial role in making 12. Bxf6⁇ a blunder, as it allowed the e-file to be opened with ...exf6, which resulted in the knight on e5 being pinned. White should have been aware of this alignment in the initial position to evaluate whether Bxf6 was safe or a blunder.

Generally, alignments are a key tactical element, especially when the king and opposing pieces are aligned on the same file or diagonal, even if there are other pieces or pawns in between. However, alignments don’t necessarily have to involve the king—they can include other pieces as well, which we'll explore in later examples.

This example teaches us the importance of recognizing potential alignments and understanding how they can influence the safety and effectiveness of our moves. By incorporating 'Alignment' into our blunder checklist, we can better anticipate the dangers that arise from such configurations.

In addition to the 'Alignment' ('A') concept, this example illustrates a broader lesson about how different checklist questions can be relevant—or not—in determining whether a move is a blunder. Specifically, the checklist questions 'Can they attack my newly-moved piece?' and 'What am I unprotecting now?' were not particularly useful in this scenario for evaluating whether 12. Bxf6 was a blunder. Instead, this example ties into a theme we've explored before: the impact of pawn captures and the new avenues they create. When White played 12. Bxf6⁇, it allowed Black to recapture with the pawn, opening the e-file. This newly opened file became extremely relevant due to the alignment between Black's rook and the white king.

As we've noted, all pawn captures create new files or diagonals, which directly connect to the Alignment part of our checklist. This reinforces the importance of considering how pawn captures can change the dynamics of the board, especially when they open up lines that could be exploited by your opponent. By being mindful of these potential alignments and the avenues created by pawn captures, you can avoid blunders and better anticipate your opponent's tactics. } ) ) *

