{ Here, Black has an extra pawn, but their pieces are sleeping on the kingside. Now they see a tactical opportunity with ...Bxc5 dxc5 ...Rd8, winning back the piece by pinning the bishop on d5. Can you please check the safety of this move ...Bxc5? Should Black play ...Bxc5 or ...Be7 here? } 22... Be7! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rf8c5,Rh1c1,Rc1c7]} { Not falling for temptation and being mindful of the alignment on the c-file! Black keeps the advantage due to having an extra pawn. } ( 22...Bxc5 $4 { Such an instructive blunder! This move looks great at first sight, as if White recaptures the bishop, they will lose the bishop on d5 after ...Rd8, netting Black two extra pawns. The problem is that we are not playing checkers, we are playing chess. } 23.Rc1! { The cold shower! White does not fulfill our dreams and wishes, but instead pins our bishop on c5, highlighting the alignment issue Black just created on the c-file by capturing the pawn on c5. } 23...Kd6 24.Rxc5 $18 { White won the bishop and obtained a winning position. } { 22...Bxc5 $4 23.dxc5? Black was wishing and hoping for this before capturing the pawn on c5. 23...Rd8 $19 It is Black who is winning now as the pinned bishop on d5 will drop on the next turn. } { What did we learn from this example?

On the psychological side, this example highlights how wishful thinking can lead to blunders. When we spot a promising idea, it’s easy to get excited and become overly optimistic, which can cloud our judgment. In this case, 22...Bxc5⁇ initially appeared to be a brilliant tactical move that could win the game if White simply recaptured the bishop. However, precisely at that moment, Black needed to be extra cautious, double-checking everything.

When you believe your move might win the game, it’s crucial to take extra time to verify it, because if your move is indeed winning, the game will soon be over, justifying the extra time spent.

The second key lesson involves the 'A' (Alignment) part of the checklist and how it relates to capturing a pawn. Before the black bishop captured the pawn on c5, the alignment on the c-file wasn’t an issue for Black's king, as the white pawn on c5 blocked the c-file. However, the moment Black captured the pawn on c5, the c-file opened up, making the position of the black king vulnerable due to Rc1, which pinned the bishop on c5.

In previous examples, we discussed how capturing with pawns can open new files and diagonals that need careful consideration. This example expands that idea to include capturing a pawn as well. When a pawn is captured, new files or diagonals inevitably clear up, potentially creating alignment issues. The white pawn on c5 had been blocking the white rook’s direct access to the black king on c7, but once the black bishop captured the pawn, the rook gained more direct access, creating a dangerous alignment.

Finally, the broader checklist question of 'Can they attack my newly moved piece?' was highly relevant in this scenario, as the bishop was directly attacked—and pinned—after it moved to c5. } ) 
