12. Bh6 $4  { Next, let's discuss the Knight forks and Pawn forks. These two types are highlighted because they rely on unique geometrical patterns that deserve focused attention. Forks (or double attacks) involving other pieces like queens, rooks, and bishops are addressed separately in this course under the categories of 'Loose Pieces' and 'Alignments.'

Before executing our move, we must ensure we don't fall victim to a deadly fork. To do this, we need to understand the geometrical patterns that make forks possible. Let's start with knight forks. In this position, White thought they found an ingenious move, activating their sleeping bishop on c1 by creating a checkmate threat: } { Although this move at first sight looks aggressive and strong, it is a blunder as it allows a deadly knight fork: } ( 12.Bg5 { This would be a better move for White, as it does not allow a knight fork that wins material. } ) Nf5 $19  {[%cal Rf5h6,Rf5g3,Rf5g7][%csl Rh6,Rg3,Rg7,Gf5]} { This multi-purpose knight fork is decisive for Black, as the knight not only targets the queen on g3 and the bishop on h6 but also defends g7, preventing checkmate. Once the white queen moves, Black will win the bishop on h6. White, focused solely on their own plan with 12. Bh6⁇, failed to consider whether that move would expose them to a deadly fork. The broader checklist question, 'Can my newly moved piece be attacked?' would have been particularly useful in this situation.

This puzzle also highlights the importance of tactical awareness, especially regarding the geometry of knight forks. Knights change the color of the square they occupy with each move, and knight forks occur when two or more of our pieces end up on the same color square as the enemy knight (as seen with the bishop on h6, queen on g3, and the knight on e7). This positioning allows the knight to jump to an opposite-colored square (such as f5 in this example), thereby attacking our pieces on the opposite color (the queen and the bishop).

This pattern means we must be especially vigilant about knight forks when our move positions multiple pieces on the same color square as the enemy knight. White overlooked this geometrical pattern before playing 12. Bh6⁇.

Another challenge in this example is that 12. Bh6 initially appeared to be a strong multi-purpose move, as it developed the bishop, connected the rooks, and gained a tempo by attacking the pawn on g7. However, many players fall into wishful thinking and confirmation bias in such moments. Instead of searching for potential countermeasures, they look for confirming evidence, leading to oversights like the punishing 12...Nf5! in this case. } 
