{ Here, I want to provide practical rules of thumb and mental shortcuts to help you avoid knight forks. Understanding knight geometry is important, and in this position, we see an example of what Karsten Müller calls the ‘knight check shadow.’

Knight Check Shadow

Knights struggle to reach squares that are two squares away diagonally. In fact, it takes a minimum of four moves for a knight to jump from its current location to a square in its knight check shadow. This means that placing your pieces at this diagonal distance from an enemy knight keeps them at a safe distance.

At first glance, careful readers might notice an apparent contradiction—the white king is on the same color as the enemy knights, which we previously mentioned as a red flag for potential knight forks. However, the knight check shadow is a key exception to this general rule, offering a surprisingly safe distance.

The Fianchetto Knight Problem

Now, let’s turn our attention to the knight on b7, which is severely restricted and dominated by the white king. Fianchetto knights often struggle in chess, as they can be trapped with limited mobility.

Visualize that all the other knights leave the board, and only the b7-knight remains. This knight would have only two safe squares: d8 and a5, forming a specific geometry known as ‘Karpov Distance’—another safe distance that we will examine in the next position. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Gd5b7,Gd5f7,Gd5f3,Gd5b3][%csl Gd5,Rb7,Rf7,Rf3,Rb3]} *

