1. -- { Here, we see what Karsten Müller calls the ‘Karpov Distance’ between the white king and four black knights—a concept named after Anatoly Karpov, who famously won many games by restricting enemy knights using this precise geometry.

Karpov Distance occurs when a knight and an enemy piece (in this case, the white king) are placed three squares apart, either vertically or horizontally. We can observe this with the knights on a5, d8, g5, and d2 in relation to the white king.

This safe distance is highly effective against knights because it takes a minimum of three moves for a knight to reach a piece placed at Karpov Distance. Unlike the Knight Check Shadow, which breaks the usual color-square pattern, Karpov Distance follows the general tendency—the white king and black knights are placed on opposite-colored squares.

Take note of the knights on a5 and d8—they are positioned on the rim, making them particularly ineffective. If these knights stood alone against the white king, it would take at least four moves for them to reach the central d5-square. In contrast, the knights on d2 and g5 can reach d5 in just three moves.

Careful observers may recognize the Karpov Distance is also a domination distance between a bishop and a knight. If we replace the white king with a white bishop, and leave only the black knight on d8, the bishop would completely dominate the knight, controlling all its escape squares. This is an essential strategic pattern that frequently appears in bishop vs. knight endgames.

Connecting Knight Check Shadow and Karpov Distance

There is an interesting relationship between Knight Check Shadow and Karpov Distance. It only takes one move for a knight to shift from Karpov Distance to Knight Check Shadow. For instance, if the knight on d8 was alone against the white king, it would have to move to b7 or f7, creating a Knight Check Shadow position. } {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Gd5d8,Gd5g5,Gd5d2,Gd5a5][%csl Gd5,Rd8,Ra5,Rg5,Rd2]} *

