{ Finally, this position highlights dangerous knight alignments—providing quick rules of thumb when a knight is in close proximity. The white king is surrounded by eight black knights, and we must determine which knights pose the greatest risk for a fork.

The most dangerous knights are those placed diagonally from the white king: c6, e6, c4, and e4. Indeed, as a general rule of thumb, pieces positioned just one square diagonally from a knight are highly vulnerable to attacks. These knights sit on the same color square as the white king. It takes only one move for them to deliver a check, making them highly dangerous in fork scenarios.

In contrast, the knights placed one square horizontally or vertically to the white king, the ones on c5, e5, d4, and d6, require at least two moves to deliver a check. While they are still more dangerous than knights at Knight Check Shadow or Karpov Distance, they pose less immediate threats than the diagonally placed knights. } 1. -- {[%mdl 32768]} {[%csl Rc6,Re6,Rc4,Re4,Ge5,Gd6,Gc5,Gd4]} *

