18... h6 {[%mdl 32768]} 
19. Nh3! { Let's introduce the 'M' in our checklist with a simple example. 'M' stands for mobility restrictions (trappable pieces). The knight on g5 is attacked. Where should it move? } {[%cal Rg5h7][%csl Gh3,Rh7]} { The only safe square. White avoids the blunder with this retreat. Here, the knight is still on the rim, but it is safe! } ( 19.Nh7 $4 { This active-looking move would be a blunder, as the knight has no return route after } 19...Rf7 { The knight gets trapped. White should have asked themselves whether their newly moved piece could be attacked on its new square and whether the knight, venturing deep into enemy territory, could get trapped there.

What did we learn from this example?

We should develop a keen eye for spotting potentially 'trappable' pieces. A clear warning sign is when a piece has limited mobility on its new square. We must ensure that, as a result of our move, none of our pieces will be left with limited options if the opponent decides to attack.

It's important to note that a piece doesn't have to be unprotected to be trappable. Even a well-guarded piece can be at risk if its mobility is restricted on a particular square. When it’s attacked by a lower-value piece, it might be forced to retreat, regardless of its protection. Dan Heisman refers to this concept as 'AWL' (Attack with something Worth Less) , which is especially crucial when it comes to trapping pieces. } ) *

