33... Qxb4 {[%mdl 32768]} 
{ How should White recapture this queen on b4? } 34. cxb4 $10  {[%cal Ra7a5][%csl Ga3,Gb4]} { Avoiding the blunder by not allowing an unstoppable passed pawn. This pawn endgame is equal. } ( 34.axb4 $4 { This would be a blunder as White allows Black to create an unstoppable passed pawn on the next move: } 34...a5! { Thanks to White's blunder, Black creates an outside passed pawn. And nobody can stop the a-pawn anymore!

White allowed this on the previous move by changing the pawn structure. } 35.bxa5 bxa5 { And the white king is not in the square of the a-pawn, which means Black will soon promote that pawn.

The Rule of the Square is a quick calculation method used to determine whether a king can catch a passed pawn before it promotes. Imagine a square extending from the passed pawn to its promotion rank, with its width equal to the number of remaining moves to promote. If the opposing king can step into this square on their turn, they can stop the pawn in time. If not, the pawn promotes. } 36.c4 a4 37.c5 a3 { The black king could have stopped the c-pawn with ...Kf8, but directly pushing the pawn also wins: } 38.c6 a2 39.c7 a1=Q+ { And Black promotes with check, thus winning the game.

What can we learn from this example?

This position highlights the critical importance of passed pawns, particularly in the endgame where they often become decisive. In endgames, it's essential to ensure that our moves do not allow the opponent to create an unstoppable passed pawn.

Endgames are highly concrete, and a single mistake can irreversibly change the outcome of the game. This risk is especially acute when a passed pawn becomes what Nimzowitsch famously called a 'criminal'—a pawn that poses a severe threat. Our objective is to keep passed pawns under lock and key or to prevent the opponent from creating one in the first place. Achieving this requires familiarity with fundamental endgame concepts, such as the rule of the square and pawn breakthroughs. } ) *

