7. dxe6 {[%mdl 32768]} { White just captured on e6. How should Black recapture this pawn? } dxe6! {[%cal Rf7e6,Rd1h5,Rh5e8,Rh5e5][%csl Re5]} { The only safe recapture! The alternative recapture would allow a deadly check on h5, picking up the loose bishop on e5. } ( 7...fxe6 $4 { This recapture allows a devastating check on the newly opened h5-e8 diagonal: } 8.Qh5+ { And White picks up the loose bishop on the next turn. Here we combine the checklist letters of C and L: Black had a loose bishop on e5, and recapturing on e6 with the f7-pawn would allow a devastating check with Qh5+, creating a double attack on the king and the loose bishop on e5. } 8...g6 9.Qxe5 { White gets a winning position with the extra knight. } ) 
8. Bb5+ { Black's last move allows this check, which I hope you have properly checked before deciding on your last move. } ( 8.Qxd8+ { This is not an issue for Black as after } 8...Kxd8 { The king is safe and well-placed in the center, getting ready for the endgame. Black has the bishop pair and a slight advantage. } ) Bd7! {[%cal Gd8d7,Ge8d7][%csl Ge8,Gd8,Gd7]} { Safely interposing with the bishop. The bishop on d7 is attacked twice and defended twice, thus Black is not losing material. } ( 8...Ke7 { This is also a safe move, as the king keeps guarding the queen on d8. However, White can play } 9.Qe2 $14 { and claim that the black king is not so safe in the middle of the board as long the queens remain on the board. } ) 
9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 
10. Qxd7+ Kxd7 $10  {[%csl Gd7]} { The black king is well placed in the center, preparing for the endgame. Black has no problems whatsoever in this ending.

What did we learn from this example?

The most instructive part of this example was how we combined 'C' (Checks) and 'L' (Loose Pieces) in the checklist. The move 7...fxe6⁇ was a blunder because it allowed a check by the white queen that also targeted our loose bishop on e5. This highlights why loose pieces are always relevant in chess—they represent a tactical weakness that can be exploited by your opponent.

Another important lesson from this example is how pawn captures create new open files and diagonals. We should be mindful of these newly created avenues before making a pawn capture. It’s easy to overlook these changes because pawn structures tend to be stable and don’t change as frequently as other elements in chess. This psychological factor can lead to blunders when players fail to recognize the new opportunities that open up as a result of a pawn move or capture.

For instance, notice how 7...dxe6 opened up both the d-file and the a4-e8 diagonal, giving White new possibilities. Black needed to be aware of these changes before making the move 7...dxe6.

So, when making a pawn capture, it's crucial to ask yourself: 'What new avenues are created as a result of this pawn capture?'. Which files and diagonals are opening up as a result of this move?'. By asking these questions, you can better anticipate potential threats and opportunities that arise from changes in the pawn structure, helping you avoid blunders and improve your overall tactical awareness. } 
