Black has an extra knight but White has a very advanced c7-pawn that is on the verge of promoting to a queen. How should White respond to this rook check? } 1. Kf1! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rg5f3][%csl Rf3]} { The only winning move! White is mindful of the Arabian mating pattern [https://www.chess.com/terms/arabian-mate-chess], as well as the perpetual check pattern involving a rook and a knight. } ( 1.Kh1 $4 { This loses due to } 1...Nf3! { Black creates the threat of Arabian mate with ...Rh2+ and White has no response against it! Notice the restricted mobility of the white king in the corner and the strength of the rook on the 2nd rank. } 2.c8=Q Rh2 { The Arabian Mate is one of the most elegant and timeless checkmating patterns in chess. In this pattern, the knight positions itself two squares diagonally from the enemy king, both restricting its escape and supporting the rook in delivering checkmate.

Again, please notice that Kh1⁇ was a blunder not because it allowed a direct check but rather that it allowed an inevitable checkmate threat. The danger signs were the restricted mobility of the white king and the deadly combination of the rook and a knight. } ) ( 1.Kg1? { This allows a perpetual check after } 1...Nf3+ 2.Kf1 Nh2+ { This typical drawing mechanism involves a rook on the 2nd/7th rank and a knight. } 3.Ke1 Nf3+ 4.Kf1 ( 4.Kd1 $4 { White has to repeat as this move allows a checkmate! } 4...Rd2 ) Nh2+ 5.Kg1 Nf3+ 6.Kf1 $10 { The game ends in a draw with threefold repetition. } ( 6.Kh1 $4 { Again, White has to repeat with Kf1 as this move allows the Arabian mate: } 6...Rh2 ) ) Nf3 { This is the most testing move. Now Black wants to go for the drawing mechanism with ...Nh2+. } 
2. Rc3! {[%cal Gf1e1,Ge1d1,Gd1c1,Gc3f3,Gc7c8][%csl Gc1,Gc3]} { The only winning move! White elegantly vacates the c1-square for the king while also hitting the knight. } ( 2.Rc6? { Any other move with the rook on the c-file would allow } 2...Rd2! { The typical drawing mechanism is set! ...Nh2+ and ...Nf3+ will follow now. } 3.c8=Q ( 3.Rc3 { It is too late to play this move now: } 3...Nh2+ 4.Kg1 Nf3+ 5.Rxf3? { White cannot capture this knight as it allows } 5...Rc2 $19 { Black stops and captures the c7-pawn and achieves a winning rook ending. } ) Nh2+ 4.Kg1 Nf3+ 5.Kf1 { White has to accept the perpetual check as } ( 5.Kh1 $4 { allows the Arabian mate: } 5...Rh2 ) 5...Nh2+ 6.Ke1 Nf3+ 7.Kf1 $10 { Black achieves the draw. } ) Nh2+ 
3. Ke1 { And the king starts escaping towards the queenside, not giving a perpetual check to Black. } Nf3+ 
4. Kd1 Rd2+ 
5. Kc1 $18  {[%cal Gc7c8]} { Black has no more checks left, and the pawn on c7 is promoting on the next turn. White wins. } *

