35. Rxh6 $4  { Taking the bait. This move is a terrible blunder as it creates a loose piece on h6. But how can Black punish it? } ( 35.Kf3! { White should have prudently blockaded the passed pawn. Now the h6-pawn is really hanging. } 35...Rh8 36.a4 { White is objectively winning, but the winning plan will proceed slowly. This move aims to create a second weakness, this time on the queenside, with a4-a5. Black's pieces are all tied down. } 36...a5 { Stopping White's idea will result in a weak pawn on b6, giving White ideas such as d6-d7 followed by Rxb6. White must proceed patiently, but the win is secured. } ) f3! {[%cal Gf3f2][%csl Rh6,Gf3,Gf8,Gf7]} { The criminal just moves forward! White can no longer blockade this passed pawn. } ( 35...Kg7 $4 { Changing the move order would be a blunder, as it allows a check: } 36.Rg6+! { A tempo-gaining check that allows blockading the passer. } 36...Kh7 37.Kf3 { Back to business. White is winning as Black's only counterplay has been stopped. } ) 
36. Kxf3 ( 36.Kg5 { Petrosian tried this move in the actual game, but Korchnoi found the only winning move: } 36...Ke8 $19 { The rook on f8 is perfectly placed to support the passed pawn on f3. White has no perpetual check and they cannot blockade the pawn. Black wins. } ) Kg7+ {[%cal Gg7h6,Gf8f3][%csl Rh6]} { This discovered check wins the loose rook on h6. Now we understand why Korchnoi played 34...Rf8. It was a sneaky trap, and the great prophylactic player Petrosian fell for it by grabbing the pawn on h6, thus creating a loose piece.

What did we learn from this example?

Firstly, it shows the true challenge of converting a winning position into a victory. Even a World Champion can falter in a favorable position, as Petrosian did, resigning just a few moves later.

This example also highlights the critical role of passed pawns in endgames, reminding us of Nimzowitsch’s advice to keep them under strict control. The move ...Rf8 should have set off alarm bells for Petrosian, known for his uncanny ability to sense danger long before it materializes.

Moreover, this scenario brings together two key elements from our checklist: L (Loose Pieces) and P (Passed Pawns). White's downfall stemmed from creating a loose piece on h6, which Korchnoi expertly exploited with the assistance of his passed pawn on f4. } 
