21. Qd6 { Let's combine the letters L, A, and P in our checklist. White just offered a queen trade on d6. How should Black respond? } Qe8 $10  {[%cal Ge8c8,Gc8c2,Re5d6][%csl Gc8,Ge8,Gc2]} { The only move that keeps the balance! Black avoids the creation of an annoying passed pawn on d6 and also keeps the c8-rook protected, mindful of the alignment on the c-file. The bishop on c2 is a loose piece, but on the next turn it can move, as the rook on c8 is protected by the queen. } ( 21...Qxd6 $4 { Creating two big problems at once: This move allows the creation of a passed pawn on d6, which is on the verge of reaching d7, hitting the rook. The second problem is that the rook on c8 is now becoming a loose piece. And this is very relevant because of the alignment on the c-file and the fact that the bishop on c2 is both loose and pinned. } 22.exd6 { White achieves a winning position as d7 will follow next, removing the defender of the loose and pinned bishop on c2. } 22...Kf8 { Black has no way to prevent d7 or unpin the bishop on c2. } 23.d7! { The annoying passed pawn won't be able to promote to a queen this time, but it will allow White to win the bishop, since the rook on c8 must let go of their defensive duty now to stop the pawn. } 23...Rd8 24.Rxc2 $18 { White wins the bishop and secures a decisive advantage. } { 21...Qc7 $4 This would allow White to exploit the pin on the c-file: 22.Qxc7 Rxc7 23.Bd3 White pins and wins. }{ 21...Qc6 $4 is also a blunder as White can put pressure on the pinned bishop with 22.Bd3! Black is losing material by force. }{ What did we learn from this example?

This position offers an instructive blend of the 'L' (Loose pieces), 'A' (Alignment), and 'P' (Passed pawns) principles from our checklist. Black had a loose bishop on c2, and the white rook on c1 was perfectly aligned with the black rook on c8 along the c-file. When Black played 21...Qxd6⁇ , the queen's disappearance turned the previously protected rook on c8 into a loose piece, making the bishop on c2 pinned—any movement of the bishop would have resulted in the loss of the rook on c8. This made the alignment on the c-file even more critical.

Furthermore, by exchanging queens on d6, Black allowed the creation of a passed pawn on d6, which became a significant distraction. This forced the black rook to abandon its defensive role over the pinned bishop on c2. This decision underscores why piece trades are highly committal; we should always strive to exchange pieces on our own terms.

The checklist principles don't operate in isolation—they interact and influence each other. This example vividly demonstrates this interconnectedness. } ) 
