{ It seems like Black can capture the pawn on d2. What would be the correct way? } 18... Nxd2! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rd8d2,Rf1c4,Re1d2][%csl Rb3]} { The safest capture—and the best move. The black knight, protected by the rook, simultaneously attacks the rook on b1 and the bishop on f1. } ( 18...Rxd2 $4 { The alternative capture is a blunder as Black creates a loose rook on d2, that is only supported by the loose knight on b3. This means the seeds for tactical destruction are planted: } 19.Bc4 $18 { The tactical destruction of Black's fragile setup. This move hits the loose knight, which cannot move as it needs to guard the loose rook on d2. The rook has no way to guard the knight. White wins material and gets a winning position. } ) ( 18...O-O { Ignoring the pawn on d2 would still give Black an advantage, though significantly less than in the main line. } 19.Rd1 { White guards the pawn and Black misses the chance to win a clear pawn. } ) 
19. Rd1 {[%cal Gd1d8]} { You must have calculated this response by White, as Black created an alignment on the d-file with their last move. } Nxf1! {[%cal Ge8d8][%csl Ge8,Gd8]} { The only move that gives Black a clear edge! The rook on d8 is protected by the black king, thus the knight could move. } ( 19...Nb3? { An instructive mistake, as the knight is becoming a loose piece and White exploits this after } 20.Rxd8+ Kxd8 21.Bc4! { Creating a double attack on the loose knight as well as the loose pawn on f7. } 21...Nc5 22.Bxf7 $10 { White achieves equality by restoring material balance. } ) 
20. Rxf1 ( 20.Rxd8+ Kxd8 21.Rxf1 Kc7 { Black gets a clear edge with an extra pawn and ...Rd8 is coming on the next turn. } ) Ke7 $17  {[%cal Gh8d8]} { Black has a clear advantage in this ending with an extra pawn.

