{ The material is equal in this endgame, but White wants to play Nc8 on the next move, to win a pawn on the queenside. How should Black deal with this Nc8 threat? } 28... b5! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rd6c8][%csl Ra7,Rb6]} ( 28...a6 $4 { This move looks logical at first sight, as Black indeed stops the Nc8 threat. However, this move is a blunder as the b6-pawn becomes unprotected now. } 29.Nc4! { Punishing the blunder!

Since Black's last move created a weak pawn on b6, the white knight returns to c4, creating a double attack on b6 and e5. White is winning a pawn by force now: } 29...Bb5 30.Nxe5! { The correct pawn to be captured! The knight defends the loose bishop on d3 this way. } 30...Bxd3 31.Nxd3 { White has a winning edge in this knight ending with an extra pawn and a better pawn structure. } { 28...a5 $4 is also a big mistake as it weakens the b6-pawn and allows 29.Nc4 Black loses one of the pawns on the queenside. } { Every pawn move weakens something, so asking 'what am I unguarding now?' is almost always crucial when advancing pawns. It's also vital to be mindful of all loose pawns and pieces across the board. The vulnerability of the e5-pawn as a loose pawn was particularly relevant in this blunder. } ) 
29. Nc8 { This move is no longer dangerous as moving the a-pawn won't drop the pawn on b6. } a5 { Black saves the a-pawn and avoids losing material.

