19... hxg3 
{ White has an extra piece in this position. Now Black captured the pawn on g3. How should White recapture this pawn? } 20. hxg3! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rg5f4,Gf1h1]} { The only move that wins! White maintains control of the f4-square and does not allow ...Bf4 ideas. The h-file is now opened, but Black cannot exploit it, as White will soon challenge that file with Rh1. } ( 20.Qxg3 $4 { This move aims to defend the kingside with the queen. The problem is that queens are the worst defenders, as they can be easily chased away by lower-valued pieces —essentially every other piece and even pawns on the chessboard! } 20...Bf4! { Ouch! Black attacks the recently moved queen, highlighting why queens are the worst defenders in chess. This move also targets the h2-pawn, preparing ...Rxh2+ ideas. Black obtains a winning attack: } 21.Qg4 Rxh2+ 22.Kg1 Rdh8 { Simply doubling up on the h-file, introducing mate in 2 threat with ...Rh1+ followed by ...R8h2+. White has no defense (the engine gives Qc8+ as the best move!) } ) ( 20.Kxg3 $4 { leads to a mate in 2: } { Qf4+ 21.Kg2 Qxh2# } ) Qh6 { This move had to be anticipated before 20. hxg3, as Black is now creating a mate-in-1 threat with ...Qh2+ on the recently opened h-file. } 
21. Rh1 $18  {[%cal Rh6h2,Gh1h6][%csl Rh2,Gh1]} { Challenging the file and preventing the mate. White has an extra piece, thus they don't mind piece trades. The white king is very secure now and White has a winning edge.

The problem with 20. Qxg3⁇ was that it created a loose square on f4, thus inviting ...Bf4 (notice the g3-pawn was keeping this option away from Black). The broader checklist question of 'Can they attack my newly moved piece?' could also be very helpful here to prevent this blunder.

