11. Nxg6 $18  {[%cal Gh5e8,Gh5h8,Rh7g6][%csl Rh8]} { The stock standard response, winning the game. The knight cannot be captured due to the loose rook on h8. White has a winning position.

This example teaches us two important lessons:

  * A piece can be active but very vulnerable at the same time, as the knight on e5 in this example. Despite the dominant appearance, the knight could get trapped within two moves as the retreat squares were controlled by the e4-pawn.

  * We should never make automatic moves in chess. 99% of players would recapture on d2 with the queen without thinking, as it looks insane to break the castling rights with 9. Kxd2. But safety is more important than those positional considerations and we must always blunder-check our moves.

