{ Black just captured the knight on c3. Should White capture on c3 or insert Bxf5 first? } 13. Qxc3! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Gc3d3][%csl Rd3]} { The only safe recapture. The queen guards the bishop on d3 from this square. } ( 13.Bxf5 $4 { Inserting this move would be a blunder as it allows a very strong intermediate check: } 13...Ne2+! { Punishing the blunder. The white bishop no longer controls the e2-square, and the black knight takes advantage of that with this check. } 14.Kh1 Nxc1! { Another important intermediate move. The knight captures the bishop on c1 while also hitting the queen on b3. White must thus react to this move and they have no time to save their f5-bishop. } 15.Raxc1 gxf5 $19 { When the smoke clears, Black ends up with an extra piece and a winning position. } ) ( 13.bxc3 $4 { Recapturing with the pawn would turn the loose bishop on d3 into a hanging piece, as the queen on b3 no longer protects this piece: } 13...Bxd3 $19 ) Rc8 { This move should have been anticipated by White too. } 
14. Qb3 {[%cal Rb3d3][%csl Gb3,Rd3]} { The queen cannot be attacked on this square while she is guarding the d3-bishop. White is in the game. } ( 14.Qd2 { This was another safe alternative for White. The queen cannot be attacked on this square while she is guarding the loose bishop on d3. } ) ( 14.Qa3 { This was yet another safe move for White: the queen keeps an eye on the d3-bishop and she cannot be attacked on this square. } ) { CLAMP Concept: Checks (C) and Loose Pieces (L) } *

