{ How should the bishop on f6 react against this attack? } 20... Be7! {[%mdl 32768]} { The only move! The d4-pawn was surprisingly poisonous. } ( 20...Bxd4 $4 { At first sight, this looked like a safe move, as Black had 3 attackers on the d4-pawn while White had only two defenders. However, White has a surprising blow: } 21.Rxd4 $3 { Preserving the deadly bishop on b2. } 21...Rxd4 22.Qe5 $18 { White creates beautiful coordination along the a1-h8 diagonal, pinning the loose rook on d4. If the rook moves, White would deliver checkmate with Qxg7+, so White is guaranteed to win the rook on the next move, securing a decisive advantage.

Black’s key challenge was to recognize the g7-pawn as a potential weakness once the dark-squared bishop is exchanged. However, the cognitive bias known as the Einstellung Effect may have played a role here.

The Einstellung Effect is a cognitive bias where prior experience and familiar patterns prevent us from seeing better, unconventional solutions. In chess, this often leads players to calculate standard ideas reflexively, overlooking unexpected but stronger moves hidden in the position.

Typically, we expect the opponent to initiate exchanges starting with the lowest-valued piece—in this case, the bishop on b2. This mental shortcut likely caused Black to overlook the possibility of capturing on d4 with the rook on the very first move. } ) { CLAMP Concept: Loose Pieces (L) } 
