{ In this sharp position, can Black capture on d4? } 19... Qxd4! {[%mdl 32768]} { It looks like an impossible move, but it is the best one! Congratulations if you have properly blunder-checked for 3(!) different White responses that look like a refutation. } 
20. Bxh7+ { This is the first refutation attempt. White wants to exploit the alignment on the d-file that Black has just created. } ( 20.e3 { The second blunder-check. Black's last move gave this pawn fork option to White. What should we do? } 20...Nxd3+! { Again, you must have seen this move before capturing on d4. The knight survives with a check. } 21.cxd3 Qf6 $17 { Black has an extra pawn and White's play on the g-file is not too dangerous. h6 will be answered by ...g6, keeping things closed. } ) ( 20.Rg4 { The third blunder check. This is actually White's best move here. Black's last move also created this alignment on the 4th rank. And White wants to exploit it with this move. } 20...Nxd3+! { Again, the knight survives with this check against the white king. You must have seen this move before capturing on d4 too! } 21.exd3 Qe5 $10 { A very sharp battle is still ahead, due to the kings being on opposite flanks. But Black has full equality and they have snatched a central pawn in the process. } ) Kxh7! 
21. Qxd4 { Ouch, did we just lose the queen for nothing? } ( 21.Rxg7+ { This is a tricky attempt from White. } 21...Qxg7! ( 21...Kxg7 $4 { This blunder loses the queen with a check. } 22.Qxd4+ $18 ) 22.Qxf4 Qh6 $19 { This forces a queen trade and Black is winning with an extra rook. } ) Nxe2+! {[%cal Ge2d4,Ge2c1][%csl Gc1,Gd4]} { Beautiful if you have seen it from the initial position! Otherwise, you just got lucky. } 
22. Kd2 Nxd4 $19  { When the smoke clears, Black ends up with an extra knight and a winning position. This example once again shows the importance of not giving up on our ideas too easily, just because they look like blunders at first sight. ...Qxd4 indeed looked like an insane move at first glance, as it would create two loose pieces and two different alignments (on the 4th rank and the d-file). And yet, concrete - and short - calculation proved Black can safely take on d4.

