{ White has an extra pawn in this knight ending. Now the black king just attacked the knight on c4. What should White play? } 30. b3! {[%mdl 32768]} {[%cal Rc5c4][%csl Gc4,Rc5]} { The only move that wins! The knight on c4 is an excellent piece, blockading the c5-pawn from rolling as well. This move both protects the knight and prevents the ...c4 followed by ...c3 idea. } ( 30.Nb6+ $4 { White wants to exchange the knights and transition into a pawn endgame with an extra pawn. But this is a blunder as it allows the creation of an unstoppable passed pawn: } 30...Nxb6 31.Kxb6 c4! { This is why context is everything in chess! White handwaved and exchanged the knights, thinking they would easily win the pawn endgame with an extra pawn. Now ...c3 cannot be prevented and Black is winning once the passed pawn is created. } 32.f3 c3 33.bxc3 bxc3 $19 ) ( 30.Nd2? { This move is a mistake as it allows the c5-pawn to roll forward: } 30...c4! { Now c3 is an annoying idea. White needs to play accurately to save the draw. } ) ( 30.Na5? { This was a mistake due to the same idea: } 30...c4 { And ...c3 is coming next, creating a dangerous passer. White needs to play accurately to survive. } ) ( 30.Kc7? { This attempts to enter a pawn endgame with a better version, but Black will save the draw here: } 30...Kxc4 31.Kxd7 Kd3 32.b3 Ke2! 33.Kd6 Kxf2 34.Kxc5 Kxg3 35.Kxb4 Kxh4 36.a4 g5 37.a5 g4 38.a6 g3 39.a7 g2 40.a8=Q g1=Q $10 ) f5 
31. Nb6+! { Now this is a great pawn ending transition for White! Why? Because Black cannot generate a deadly passed pawn after the knight trade. } Nxb6 
32. Kxb6 {[%cal Rc5c4,Gb3c4][%csl Gc4]} c4 { Please notice that if the white pawn would still be on b2 in this position, Black would be winning as they would generate a deadly passed pawn on the next move. } 
33. bxc4+ Kxc4 
34. f3 { White has several winning moves here. } Kd3 
35. e4 fxe4 
36. fxe4 Kxe4 
37. Kc5 {[%cal Gc5b4,Ga2a4,Ga4a5][%csl Rb4]} { Now the b4-pawn is falling and White will promote to a queen first. } Kf3 
38. Kxb4 Kxg3 
39. a4 Kxh4 
40. a5 $18  {[%cal Ga5a6,Ga6a7,Ga7a8]} { White wins as they will promote to a queen much faster than Black.

One of the key takeaways from this position is that we should not blindly enter a pawn endgame even if we have an extra pawn. The asymmetrical pawn structure on the queenside should have prompted consideration of whether the opponent could create an unstoppable passed pawn following the knight trade.

