7. e5 {[%mdl 32768]} { How should Black capture the pawn on e5? } dxe5! {[%cal Gc6d8,Rd1d8][%csl Gc6,Rd8,Gd1]} { The only correct capture! Black has to be prudent regarding the subsequent opening of the d-file and see the alignment between the queens. It is very important to keep the knight on c6, as it is defending the queen on d8 and does not allow the standard trick of Bxf7+- } ( 7...Nxe5 $4 { This is a blunder as it removes the defender of the queen on d8. Due to their lack of development, Black is not ready for the opening of the d-file. } 8.Nxe5 dxe5 { Suddenly, the d-file has been opened and the black queen became a loose piece: attacked by the white queen and only guarded by the black king. } 9.Bxf7+! { Bang! This standard trick removes the defender of the loose queen on d8. Black is losing the queen on the next turn. } 9...Kxf7 10.Qxd8 $18 { Please notice that this tactic only works if none of the black rooks control the d8-square. Lack of development was beautifully punished. } ) 
8. Bxf7+? { This typical trick no longer works for White. } ( 8.Qxd8+ { This is White's best try, but Black has no problems in this position: } 8...Kxd8 9.Bxf7 e4! { Chasing the knight and introducing ...Nd4. } 10.Nh4 g5 11.Ng6 Rh7 12.Bb3 Bg7 13.g4 { White must stop ...Bf5, but the g6-knight is still misplaced. Black has a slight edge in this ending. } ) Kxf7 {[%cal Gc6d8][%csl Gd8,Gc6]} { Notice how important it was for the c6-knight to stay on that square. It is the only piece guarding the black queen now. } 
9. Qxd8 ( 9.Nxe5+ Kg7! { The c6-knight again stays put and Black moves the king away. Black is winning with an extra piece. } ) Nxd8 $19  { Black ends up in a winning position with an extra piece.

After capturing the pawn on e5, Black needed to anticipate that this move would create an open file, leading to a position where the queens would directly confront each other. Black needed to recognize the danger of leaving their queen vulnerable on d8, which could lead to a disastrous 'removing the defender' tactic for White.

Understanding this risk should have prompted Black to reject 7...Nxe5, which exchanges the defender of the black queen before the d-file opens.

CLAMP Concept: Loose Pieces (L) and Alignments (A) } 
