31. Qxh7 {[%mdl 32768]} { Here comes one of the most thrilling subvariations from the World Championship Match between Ding and Gukesh. This position nearly appeared on the board, but Gukesh opted for a different move as White. The stunning 31. Qxh7 is a true bombshell. How should Black respond to this daring move? } Qd4+ $3  {[%cal Rc5d4,Rf1f8,Rh8h7]} { Another jaw-dropping moment: the only move that avoids defeat! It’s incredibly rare in chess to witness a queen sacrifice being met with a counter-queen sacrifice—but here, it’s the sole path to victory for Black! } ( 31...Rxh7 $4 { Capturing the queen would allow a mate in 1: } 32.Rf8 { This was the most obvious point of the queen sacrifice. } ) ( 31...Qxh4 $4 { At first sight, this move looks to be safe, as capturing the rook would be answered by ...Qxh8, covering the key f8-square. However White will capture the queen instead: } 32.Qxh4! Rxh4 { This move leaves the key f8-square unprotected and allows the same mate: } 33.Rf8 ) ( 31...Kd8 $4 { This is the only other option that avoids the mate, but White will win after } 32.Qxh8+ $18 { This gives an extra rook and a safer king to White. } ) 
32. Kh2 ( 32.Bxd4 { This was the main point of the queen sacrifice: now the bishop is diverted from c5, thus it no longer controls the f8-square. } 32...Rxh7 $19 { Now the white queen can be safely captured, as Rf8+ is no longer checkmate. } 33.Bc5 { White can renew the checkmate threat, but Black has several good choices, e.g. } 33...Rh8 $19 ) ( 32.Kh1 { This will be answered in the same way as the mainline. } 32...Qxh4+ $19 ) ( 32.Rf2 Qxf2+! 33.Kxf2 Rxh7 { Black gets a winning material advantage. } ) Qxh4+! {[%cal Gh4h2]} { Again, the only move that wins the game! The point is now this move comes with a check, thus Black gains a crucial tempo. } ( 32...Rxh7 $4 33.Rf8 ) ( 32...Qe5+ $4 { This mindless check would also be a blunder, as after } 33.Kh1 $18 { Black has no more checks to give, and the rook on h8 is dropping on the next turn. } 33...g6 { Guarding the rook with this move would allow a different mate! } 34.Qe7 ) 
33. Qxh4 Rxh4+ {[%cal Gh4h2,Rf1f8][%csl Rf8]} { If this move did not come with a check, Black would be mated after Rf8+ here! } 
34. Kg3 Rh8! {[%cal Rf1f8][%csl Rf8]} { Precision until the very end! This is the sole move that secures victory, as Black must neutralize the looming Rf8+ checkmate threat. What an extraordinary defensive combination! What makes chess so profound is that all these breathtaking variations remain hidden beneath the surface!

