13. Ne5 {[%mdl 32768]} { White just moved the knight to the central square on e5. Can Black capture the knight on e5? } Nxe5! {[%cal Gd8d3][%csl Gd8,Rd3]} { Yes they can! But aren't we just blundering a pawn fork? } ( 13...Bxe5 { Black can also start the trades on e5 with the bishop, however this gives additional options to White: } 14.dxe5 Nxe5 { In the mainline, there is a black bishop on e5 instead of the knight. And that bishop is stronger as it would also hit the pawn on b2, giving White fewer options. } 15.Bf1 { Here White can preserve the bishop pair while also hitting the knight on e5. } 15...Nd3 16.Re3! Nxb2 $6 { Winning the second pawn would be risky as after } 17.Qc2 { the black knight is on the verge of getting trapped. The mainline is a clear improvement for Black. } ) 
14. dxe5 {[%cal Ge5f6,Ge5d6][%csl Rf6,Rd6,Ge5]} { At first sight, Black committed a blunder by allowing this pawn fork. I hope you saw further! } Bxe5! {[%cal Gd8d3,Gd6e5][%csl Rb2,Rd3]} { Bang! Black wins a pawn by exposing an attack against the loose bishop on d3 with their queen. The pawn fork was nothing dangerous after all! } 
15. Rxe5 { White had no good way to save the bishop on d3 by creating a counterattack. } ( 15.Bf1 { Saving the bishop would allow } 15...Bxb2 $19 { Black wins another pawn and gets a winning position. This was one of the main reasons why Black started the trades on e5 with the knight and ended with their bishop. } ) Qxd3 $17  { After a crisp 5-ply calculation, Black emerges with a clear extra pawn and a significant edge.

This position highlights the importance of recognizing alignments and loose pieces—especially when pawn structure transformations occur mid-exchange. A key detail in this puzzle: when White's knight stood on e5, the bishop on d3 was safe, supported by the knight. But the moment a trade happened on e5, the bishop became a loose piece, turning into an immediate target.

Even in the final position, it is important for Black to be aware of the loose queen on d3 and the alignment on the d-file. Fortunately, the white bishop cannot move while giving a check, picking up the black queen on the next turn.

CLAMP Concept: Loose Pieces (L) and Alignments (A) } 
