1... cxd4 
{ White has an extra piece in this endgame. Can they recapture the pawn on d4? } 2. Bxd4! {[%mdl 32768]} { Yes they can, but only if you have properly analyzed the next 7 moves! } ( 2.Bc1 Rxc2 3.Nd3 Kf7 { White still keeps the edge here but it is not nearly as decisive as the mainline. Black has two pawns for the piece and they will have fighting chances here. } ) Bxd4 
3. Rxd4 e5 {[%csl Rd4,Rf4]} { Before capturing on d4, White should have seen this geometry that invites a pawn fork. Do you see a rescue for White or are they going to lose material? } 
4. Nd5! {[%cal Gd5e7,Ge7c8,Ge7g8]} { Good tactical vision! This counterattacking move saves the knight as it prepares the fork on e7. } ( 4.Rxd7 { Entering this rook endgame with an extra pawn would not be enough to win: } 4...exf4 5.Rd2 Kf7 { Black should hold with best play. } ) exd4 ( 4...Rxc2 { White has many winning moves here, and this one looks convincing: } 5.Ne7+ Kf8 6.Rxd7 Rxb2 7.Nc6 $18 ) 
5. Ne7+! { This knight fork regains the rook. } Kf8 
6. Nxc8 { It looks like the smoke has cleared and Black must resign as they are down a knight. However, we are not done yet at all! } b3 {[%cal Rd4d3,Rd3d2,Rc2b3]} { You are a great player if you have anticipated this resource from the initial position. Now Black wants to undermine the c2-pawn in order to promote their d4-pawn. Has White just blundered an unstoppable passed pawn? } 
7. cxb3! d3 
8. Kf1! {[%cal Gf1e2,Rd3d2][%csl Gf1]} { The only winning move! The king is inside the square of the pawn. } d2 
9. Ke2 {[%csl Rd2,Ge2]} { White converts the win with the extra knight, but notice the critical detail: the knight on c8 alone couldn't stop the passed d-pawn, requiring the support of the white king. If the white king had started on h1 in the initial position, this entire sequence would fail, and capturing the pawn on d4 with the bishop would have been a blunder!

This beautifully illustrates why chess is such a concrete game, where every detail—no matter how small—can make all the difference. This example neatly combined pawn fork and passed pawn themes when we blunder-check our moves. We also trained tactical vision (the knight d5-e7 idea of saving the knight).

